{
  "_doc": "Per-event hook groups run by `.claude/hooks/dispatch.js <Event>` in WORKER THREADS of one process (see lib/hook-engine.js). Each event's `groups` are the settings.json groups that used to register these hooks (same matcher, command, timeout); order within an event is a chosen linearization (trust-state readers first, producers before checkers, auto-format last). `on_incomplete` on every row declares the row's incomplete-execution contract, and is a cross-lane interface (the Codex policy emitter consumes the same declaration): \"deny\" = the row's code path can turn the merged reply into a refusal, so it runs in a worker of its OWN (lib/hook-engine.js::runDetectorsIsolated § the per-row boundary; that boundary covers the row's realm AND the event's git-answer cache — a deny row is seeded only with answers written by deny rows) AND a deny row that does NOT complete (crash, own-budget overrun, non-zero exit without a JSON reply) REFUSES the call at PreToolUse — fail-closed, security over availability (lib/dispatch-main.js § FAIL-CLOSED FOR INCOMPLETE DENY ROWS) — `.claude/bin/check-deny-capable-declarations.mjs` reds when the declared \"deny\" set drifts from the lexed source scan, either direction; \"continue\" = advisory (shares a batch). MISSING or an unrecognised value reads as \"deny\" (lib/dispatch-registry.js::onIncompleteOf) — fail-closed. settings.json pins this file's sha256 in each dispatcher command — after ANY edit run `node .claude/bin/hook-dispatch-migrate.mjs --repin --apply`. Tooling reads the expanded view via lib/dispatch-registry.js::expandSettingsHooks. Add a new detector HERE (or at gate review), never as a new settings.json entry.",
  "schema": 1,
  "events": {
    "PreToolUse": {
      "dispatcher": {
        "matcher": "*",
        "timeout": 562,
        "_timeout_note": "Raised 457 -> 547 (2026-10-09, CODEX POLICY-TIMEOUT AUDIT): six hooks holding a Codex `can_deny` policy entry had a <=6s registered budget (adjacency-heartbeat 5, check-merge-separation-guard 6, force-push-recovery-guard 6, landing-window-guard 5 on BOTH matchers, provenance-capture-tool 5, triage-routing-guard 6). The Codex runtime fails every policy CLOSED, so a load-induced timeout there refuses the call — the class measured 2026-10-08 at 3.7-5.7s actual under host load. Each was raised to 20s with its hook's OWN internal budget moved to match (16000/20000, preserving the Rule 7 internal-before-registered shape; adjacency-heartbeat's shared constant moved to 20000 on both its rows, which is why the Stop event also moved, 97 -> 113). Arithmetic: the six raises add +102s to the serial worst case, less the 2x6s the removed operator-gate rows had contributed -> 542s required, 547 keeps the 5s reserve. Raised 451 -> 457 (2026-10-09, operator-gate root fix): `operator-gate.js` gained a `PreToolUse:Skill` row (5s budget, `on_incomplete: deny`), moving the serial worst case 451 -> 452; 457 keeps the same 5s reserve the earlier raises used. SAME CHANGE, LATER IN THE SAME DAY — the same hook's Bash and Edit|Write|NotebookEdit rows were REMOVED (they could never trigger, and on Codex their presence made a policy timeout refuse ordinary READS: the extractor fans CC matchers into the Codex policy tables and Codex fails every policy closed), taking two 5s budgets back out of the serial worst case. 457 is therefore ABOVE the current requirement by those two budgets; it is left in place rather than trimmed, because the value is re-derived on the next `hook-dispatch-migrate` run and a timeout that covers more than it must is not a defect. Shape: the serial worst case = the sum of every detector budget +1s per-detector grace, PLUS the per-row worker-boot term, PLUS the 2s reserve. Raised 258 -> 270 (2026-10-01, T68) because `landing-window-guard.js` was added to BOTH the Bash and the Edit|Write|NotebookEdit groups: the worst case went 256 -> 267, so 258 no longer covered it. 270 -> 272 (2026-10-06, hook-consolidation S3) adds the PER-ROW WORKER ISOLATION term: a row declaring `on_incomplete: \"deny\"` runs in a worker of its OWN, and that boot is charged to the EVENT deadline (a row's own budget timer starts at its `start` frame) — 18 deny rows here (17 at S3; +1 on 2026-10-06, round-6 SEC R1-F1: register-regression-guard.js declared \"deny\" once the ratchet learned the identifier-assigned severity shape). hook-dispatch-migrate.mjs::ISOLATED_BOOT_MS_PER_ROW reserves 100 ms per deny row, ~3x the measured ~36 ms per extra boot (MEASURED on this machine over the REAL PreToolUse Bash group, 16 rows / 7 deny, 6 alternating runs: 131 ms shared vs 489 ms isolated medians, outcomes byte-identical). The value is NOT pinned anywhere; hook-dispatch-registry.test.mjs asserts only that settings.json and this file AGREE, which hook-dispatch-migrate.mjs --repin --apply maintains. Raised 272 -> 297 (2026-10-07, ruling-B): genesis-anchor-guard's row budget grew 5s -> 30s under host load ~100/16 cores — it overran 5s and, declaring deny, fail-closed refused commit/push-shaped calls — moving the serial worst case 267 -> 292; 297 keeps the reserve; the 30 is itself TEMPORARY pending the round-3 fast-path fix, with this timeout to return to a measured budget in that same train. Raised 297 -> 317 (2026-10-08): validate-bash-command.js's row budget grew 10s -> 30s — its commit-vetting arm runs the synced-disclosure scan under a 20s internal cap, and under host load ~105/16 cores the scan takes 30s+ (MEASURED: 30.3s direct, guard 21.3s unhurried against its 10s budget), so it fail-closed refused a commit staging `.claude/**` paths; the 30 covers the internal ceiling plus overhead, TEMPORARY pending a fast-path (the scanner's own timeout env is not reachable from a running session). The settings.json entry read 303 by drift before this run while the fresh derivation of the pre-edit rows was 297; this re-derivation settles both to 317, and a session running when the file changes keeps its started registry until restart or hook re-review (the dispatcher's own notice). Raised 317 -> 451 (2026-10-08): NINE deny rows across the Bash and Edit|Write|NotebookEdit groups were measured at or over their 5s/6s budgets under host load ~134/16 cores and raised to 20s — direct passthroughs: signing-mutation-guard 3712ms/5686ms vs 5s (its overrun fail-closed refused an Edit), register-regression-guard 4056ms, rebase-content-loss-guard 4473ms vs 6s, posture-gate 3195ms/2853ms; raised alongside for one consistent disposition: integrity-guard, journal-write-guard, cross-ecosystem-disclosure-guard (1.9-2.4s direct, ~2.5x margin once the dispatcher's worker boot and engine overhead are added). synthetic-load-guard LEFT at 8s (1.9s measured = 4x headroom — evidence says inside budget). 20 = ~3.5x the worst measured direct passthrough. TEMPORARY pending the queued after-train guard-latency program (measured budgets + fast paths), which the same measurements seed. Raised 547 -> 562 (2026-10-09, CODEX-EDIT-LANE WIRING): `adjacency-leasecheck.js` entered the Codex `apply_patch` policy table (marker + CC edit-matcher registration, FF-AC6-1) at a 5s budget — the same fail-closed-timeout class this note records — so its budget moved to 20s with its OWN internal constant to 16000 (the Rule 7 internal-before-registered shape), adding +15s to the serial worst case 542 -> 557; 562 keeps the 5s reserve. `settings-deny-edit-guard.js` (a DIRECT settings.json entry, not a dispatcher member) moved 5s -> 20s with its internal constant to 16000 in the same change; it does not enter this arithmetic."
      },
